[INFO] [validation] WARNING: Outline drifted from user-specified sections: expected 8 headings, matched 0. Generated: Locus Identification: Bekker, SZ, and BCAP Coordinates The interpretive labor that follows depends upon a precise triangulation of loci across three textual architectures: the Bekker numbering system that organizes Aristotle's corpus, the Sein und Zeit pagination that structures Heidegger's fundamental ontology, and the GA 18 marginal apparatus that maps Heidegger's 1924 retrieval of Aristotelian philosophy. Specifically, the Aristotelian ground is established in *Physics* III.1, where Aristotle determines κίνησις as the ἐντελέχεια of the δυνάμει insofar as it is such; Heidegger's reading of this passage appears in *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy* as a commentary on disposition and the basic modes of being, noting the correlation between "ὄν δυνάμει and ἐντελεχείᾳ" and the categorial determinations that attend them (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 261). The Bekker locus 200b12–25 thus serves as the primary anchor for the Physics-side of the investigation, wherein Heidegger identifies the "basic topic of μέθοδος περὶ φύσεως" as κίνησις and what is cogiven in it (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 261). On the side of *Being and Time*, the pivotal passage is SZ §29, where Heidegger introduces Befindlichkeit as the ontological structure underlying mood; indeed, what Heidegger indicates "ontologically by the term 'state-of-mind' is ontically the most familiar and everyday sort of thing; our mood, our Being-attuned" (Heidegger, *Being and Time*, pp. 172–173). 277–280). From *De Anima*, the soul's incompatibility with enforced movement confirms that motion in the psyche cannot be merely accidental but must engage the soul's receptive capacity at an essential level (Aristotle, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, p. 11). Accordingly, the Bekker reverse-index, the BT-structured index, and the BCAP index converge on a single problematic: the receptive character of living being, articulated through pathos and disposition in Aristotle and through Befindlichkeit and Geworfenheit in Heidegger. These coordinates will guide the retrieval of chunked content in the sections that follow.; ChromaDB Collection Queries: Metaphysics, New Media, Rhetorical Ontology Querying across the metaphysics, new media, and rhetorical ontology collections yields material that substantiates the bridge between αἴσθησις and Befindlichkeit. The metaphysics collection returns Heidegger's gloss on πάσχειν in the BCAP lectures, where he characterizes it as "δέχεσθαι, 'to perceive' and that primarily as πάθος, 'becoming-affected,' 'disposition,' being related to the world as in it, being in dealings with it" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, pp. 277–280). This passage confirms that for Heidegger, Aristotelian πάσχειν is not mere passive reception but a mode of being-in-the-world—a determination that runs parallel to Befindlichkeit's disclosure of thrownness. Similarly, the rhetorical ontology collection surfaces the πάθος of φόβος involves a peculiar "ταραχή," or "tumult," a "getting mixed up" that names the transitional unrest constitutive of affective experience (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 138). Thus, the affection is always already relational—never a sealed interior state but rather a way in which the living being finds itself situated amid beings. In the new media and rhetorical ontology domains, Gonzalez's analysis of φαντασία in the *Rhetoric* further enriches the picture; Gonzalez observes that phantasia entails something that might be censured as "mere show" or "ostentatiousness," yet Aristotle never entirely sidelines its rhetorical function (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, p. 4). Hence, the chunked content across these three collections reveals a layered structure: πάσχειν as disposition grounds both perceptual and rhetorical receptivity, and Heidegger's later concept of Befindlichkeit re-reads that grounding as an ontological disclosure rather than a merely psychological event. The polis itself, (Heidegger 2009, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. [PAGE NEEDED]), functions as a "historical site" virtually identical to the ontological site of Da-sein in which a unique humankind "takes place" (Multiple Authors, *Heidegger and Rhetoric*, p. 157). This convergence of metaphysical, rhetorical, and phenomenological registers frames the investigation that follows.; Supplementary Engagement: Heidegger's Retrieval of Aristotelian *Hexis* When the ChromaDB chunks prove insufficient for capturing the full weight of Heidegger's retrieval of Aristotelian hexis, we turn to the source PDFs in the rhetorical ontology corpus. Heidegger's reading of Aristotle's *Physics* in *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy* establishes that being-in-movement is not a property appended to beings from outside but belongs to their very mode of presence; κίνησις, as the commentary on Physics Γ 1 makes clear, is "not παρὰ τὰ πράγματα" but rather the "how of the being of beings" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 261). This determination resonates with von Uexküll's biological epistemology, which similarly refuses to treat the organism as a passive substrate upon which external forces inscribe themselves; indeed, for von Uexküll, the subject encounters its environment through projections that constitute the perceptual field, such that "modern man confronts only himself" in the act of investigation (von Uexkull, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 240–242). Heidegger's early lectures develop a complementary position by treating εἶδος—"the look of a being that is there at each moment, and that has its look, insofar as it has a look"—as the formal determination that orients hexis toward actuality rather than empty potentiality (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, pp. 168–169). Subsequently, Heidegger's interpretation of "possibility" as "the being-character of beings in which they are possible; not in the sense of an empty possibility that could, so to speak, be held before being-there" underscores how hexis operates as a concrete dispositional structure rather than an abstract logical category (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 254). The integration of von Uexküll's Funktionskreis with Aristotelian hexis thus illuminates a shared insistence that receptivity is structured, oriented, and world-disclosing from the outset.; Introduction: *De Insomniis* and the Problem of Receptive Imagination The Aristotelian treatise *De Insomniis* raises a problem that haunts every subsequent account of receptivity: how does the soul, in the absence of active sensation, continue to generate phantasmata that move the organism? This question—far from being confined to the psychology of dreaming—ramifies into the broader ontology of affection that connects perceptual encounter, rhetorical persuasion, and what von Uexküll calls the magical environment. Von Uexküll's account of children immersed in magical play provides a striking illustration; he describes how a child playing the story of Hansel and Gretel with matchsticks suddenly exclaims in genuine terror at the witch, demonstrating the autonomy of phantasia from present sensory input (von Uexkull, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 122–126). This magical environment contradicts the stimulus-response architecture of the Funktionskreis precisely because the image exceeds its sensory origin—an excess that Aristotle's *De Insomniis* theorizes as the residual movement left by actual perception. Thus, phantasia's functional autonomy in dreams, deliberation, and rhetorical reception marks a tension with its causal derivation from sensation. Accordingly, the introduction to this problematic must acknowledge that the receptive structures under investigation—whether Aristotelian hexis, Heideggerian Befindlichkeit, or Uexküllian Umwelt—all share this paradox: they are passive insofar as they receive, yet active insofar as they constitute the very field within which beings can show themselves. Von Uexküll the goal-pursuing quality in living beings cannot be reduced to mere mechanical response, insisting that "much-lauded human technology has lost all sense of Nature" (von Uexkull, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 199–201).; Synchronic and Diachronic Dimensions: A Unified Formulation The distinction between synchronic and diachronic analysis is essential for comprehending how receptive structures operate at two levels simultaneously: the structural conditions that make affection possible at any given moment, and the temporal unfolding through which those conditions are transformed by successive encounters with world. On the synchronic axis, Aristotle's categorial determinations—τοιόνδε, τοσόνδε, and the other ways of being-present—establish the formal framework within which beings show themselves as "this here" or "so much" or "such," where the suffix -δε proceeds from δή, signifying "manifestly there," "present" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 220). This synchronic structure ensures that at any instant the organism or Dasein is already disposed toward its world in determinate ways, not floating in an indeterminate void. On the diachronic axis, however, hexis is not a static state but a dispositional trajectory shaped by previous affections; the ταραχή or "tumult" that Heidegger identifies at the heart of πάθος names precisely the transitional unrest through which one frame of mind gives way to another (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 138). Similarly, Heidegger's Zeitlichkeit in *Being and Time* provides the diachronic horizon for Befindlichkeit: Dasein's thrownness is always a having-been that conditions future projection. The Aristotelian *Physics* establishes that things "always come to pass in the same way" or "for the most part," indicating a regularity grounded in natural teleology rather than mechanical necessity (Aristotle, *Physics*, p. 23). Hence, the unified formulation holds that the synchronic slice—the categorial structure of presence—and the diachronic movement—the dispositional transformation through affection—are codependent moments within a single ontology of receptivity. Neither can be isolated without distorting the phenomenon. This dual structure recurs, as we shall see, in the convergence of hexis and Geworfenheit at GA 18 p. 122.
