htarrow M_4$ that *orexis* alone could not.\n\n====================================================================\nPER-PHASE REQUIREMENTS — DETAILED INSTRUCTIONS\n====================================================================\n\n**Phase A — Opening Hinge (~150 words).**\n\nPRESERVE the substance of source line 3's first sentence (\"However, this structure alone does not fully encompass the role emotion plays in animal movement as Aristotle presents it\"). Re-cast in the polished register so it functions as a transition from §1.6's closing image. Cross-reference the *Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22 judgment-affecting definition (no need to re-quote; it was quoted in §1.2 and is in working memory for the chapter).\n\nDO NOT introduce *De Insomniis* in this phase. Save the quotation for Phase B.\n\nDO NOT re-establish the linear $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$ chain. The chain is established in §1.6; reference it as \"the linear chain established in the prior section\" or \"the actualization chain $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$\" without re-development.\n\n**Phase B — The *De Insomniis* Mechanism (~600 words).**\n\nCONSOLIDATE source lines 3, 5, and 7 into a single non-redundant development. The three are substantially duplicate; the polished version draws from the cleanest formulations of each:\n\n- The opening framing uses source line 5's cleaner prose (\"The *Rhetoric*'s definition of emotion as judgment-affecting [*Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22] entails a structural feature that the linear chain cannot fully capture\").\n- The *De Insomniis* 460b3–11 quotation goes in the BODY (not a footnote), per source line 5's placement. The full quotation: \"We are easily deceived respecting the operations of sense-perception when we are excited by emotions, and different persons according to their different emotions; for example, the coward when excited by fear, the amorous person by the object of his desire; so that, with but little resemblance to go upon, the former thinks he sees his foes 