a*. This is the mechanism Aristotle explicitly specifies in *De Insomniis*; mechanism (1) is the structural complement that the *Stimmung*-saturation language brings into focus.\n  - The two are commonly conflated under \"feedback loop\" but operate at different points in the chain: (1) at the basic-valence input; (2) at the corrective-gate.\n- (iv) Both mechanisms are diachronic modulations of synchronic transitions, not new synchronic transitions. Stated formally: mechanism (1) specifies that the basic valence at $A_0$ is itself a function of prior actualizations (the basic valence brought to a perceptual encounter is shaped by prior dispositional history); mechanism (2) specifies that the *doxa*-gate's content-conditionality is itself modulated by prior actualizations (emotional excitement disables the discriminative filter, so the gate fires more readily under sustained *pathē*).\n\nDO NOT use the D1/D2/D3 notation from source lines 31–35. This notation is internal scaffolding; the polished prose should refer to \"the synchronic transitions\" and \"the apparent feedback direction\" or \"what appears to be a third structural transition\" rather than D1, D2, D3 letters. The notation may be retained internally for the writer's clarity but must not appear in the output.\n\nDO NOT re-introduce or re-quote *De Insomniis* in this phase. It was quoted and developed in Phase B; here it is referenced.\n\n**Phase E — *Hexis*/*Geworfenheit* Temporality (~700 words).**\n\nDEVELOP from source lines 13, 15, 17, 19, 21, 23, 25, 27, 29, 41, 43. The source has substantial duplication and disorganization across these lines. The polished version develops the argument in three movements:\n\n- (i) **The Aristotelian articulation: *hexis*** (~250 words). Open with *NE* II.1, 1103a14–b2. Quote: \"moral excellence comes about as a result of habit\" (1103a16–17), and the canonical formulation: \"For the things we have to learn before we can do, we learn by doing, e.g. men become builders by building and lyre-players by playing the lyre; so too we become just by doing just acts, temperate by doing temperate acts, brave by doing brave acts\" (*NE* II.1, 1103a31–b2). Argue that each actualization of a *pathos*-chain leaves a residual modification of the dispositional ground; over time, repeated actualizations consolidate into *hexeis* that condition subsequent chain-actualizations. *Hexis* is not a third stage of the chain but the temporal sedimentation of repeated chain-actualizations into a dispositional ground. Cross-reference (briefly) the *hexis*-as-how-of-*pathos* claim already established in §1.2's footnote 1 (BCAP p. 125) — do not re-quote, just refer.\n\n- (ii) **The Heideggerian articulation: *Befindlichkeit* and *Geworfenheit*** (~300 words). Open with the systematic formulation from *BT* §29. Develop the three essential characteristics of state-of-mind: (1) it discloses Dasein in its thrownness; (2) it has already disclosed Being-in-the-world as a whole, making it possible first of all to direct oneself toward something; (3) it discloses the worldhood of the world in the mode of circumspective concern (cite SZ §29, H.137; Eng. 176). State that thrownness names the diachronic persistence of prior dispositional states: Dasein is always already in a *Stimmung* that conditions its disclosure; the *Stimmung* carries forward from prior actualizations. Quote selectively from BT 174/175 the thrownness passage: \"this characteristic of Dasein's Being—this 'that it is'—is veiled in its 'whence' and 'whither', yet disclosed in itself all the more unveiledly; we call it the 'thrownness' [*Geworfenheit*] of this entity into its 'there'\" (SZ §29, H.135; Eng. 174). Develop the temporality claim from *BT* §68b: \"Understanding is grounded primarily in the future; one's *state-of-mind*, however, temporalizes itself *primarily* in *having been*. Moods temporalize themselves—that is, their specific ecstasis belongs to a future and a Present in such a way, indeed, that these equiprimordial ecstases are modified by having-been\" (SZ §68, H.340; Eng. 390). Argue that *Befindlichkeit*'s having-already-found-itself-disposed is not a past stored in memory but a *Gewesenheit* (having-been) that *currently* conditions how the world's entities can come close.\n\n- (iii) **The convergence: GA 18 *hexis* prefigures *BT* *Geworfenheit*** (~150 words). Cite BCAP p. 122 (the four basic aspects of being-there derived from *hexis*; the fourth aspect: \"Being-there must, *for itself*, take the opportunity to cultivate this being-composed as a possibility\"; *hexis*-genesis \"in being-there itself\"). Argue that the lectures' fourth aspect of being-there derived from *hexis* is the lectures' formulation of the diachronic dispositional persistence that *Geworfenheit* will name in *Being and Time*. Close with the structural thesis: the feedback is not a structurally distinct third direction but the thrownness-character of *pathos* — the temporal persistence of prior actualizations as mood-ground for subsequent encounters. The Aristotelian *hexis* and the Heideggerian *Geworfenheit* are two articulations of one phenomenon — prior dispositional actualization persists as the ground from which present disclosure takes its character — at different ontological registers (Aristotle's at the perception-and-affection level within the soul-structure; Heidegger's at the level of Dasein's fundamental disclosedness).\n\nABSORB into Phase E:\n- Source line 11's connection to *hexis* (NE II.1) and *Geworfenheit* — develop here, not in Phase D.\n- Source line 19's \"shared phenomenon\" framing — incorporate into the convergence movement (iii).\n- Source line 27's *hexis*-prefigures-*Geworfenheit* formulation with BCAP p. 122 quote — central to movement (iii).\n- Source line 29's \"what this analogy reveals\" claim — incorporate into the convergence movement (iii) and use as the closing claim of Phase E.\n- Source line 43's *NE* II.1 quotations — central to movement (i).\n\nDO NOT re-establish the SZ §29 H.138 \"first systematic hermeneutic\" citation. This is established in §1.2; cross-reference is sufficient if needed (e.g., \"the retrospective citation of *Rhetoric* II established in §1.2\"). The 1.7 source includes the H.138 quote at line 25 — it should be DROPPED from 1.7 (cross-reference §1.2 instead).\n\nDO NOT use the BCAP p. 173–174 fear-analysis material as a master case here. Phase E is about the temporality of state-of-mind in general, not fear specifically. The fear-analysis-material may be touched on in passing only if it directly supports the *Geworfenheit*-as-pathos-grade-thrownness thesis.\n\nDO NOT cite or develop *BT* §40 (anxiety) or §65 (primordial temporality of care) at length. These are deeper *BT* analyses that exceed the section's scope. §68b is the load-bearing reference for moods-temporalizing-in-having-been; if §65 is referenced, it should be in a single brief sentence as a forward-pointer.\n\n**Phase F — Closing Transition to 1.8 (~100 words).**\n\nState the section's conclusion: the *pathos*-chain is synchronically directional but diachronically saturated. The chain runs $A_0 \\rightarrow A_4$ within any given moment, but each moment's $A_0$ is itself the having-been-shaped of prior runs.\n\nTransition forward to §1.8: with the chain's synchronic directionality (established §1.5–§1.6) and diachronic temporality (established here) both specified, the analysis can now turn to the question of emotion's specific causal function within the chain — what work emotion does at $M_3 \\rightarrow M_4$ that *orexis* alone could not accomplish.\n\nDO NOT preempt §1.8's argument. The transition forward is one sentence that names §1.8's question; the argument itself belongs to §1.8.\n\n====================================================================\nINTEGRATION TABLE — SOURCE-LINE DISPOSITIONS\n====================================================================\n\n| Source loc. | Content | Disposition |\n|-------------|---------|-------------|\n| Line 3 (¶ 1) | First De Insomniis intro + 460b3–20 footnote + empty `\\hl{(CITE)}\\footnote{}` | CONSOLIDATE into Phase B; long footnote becomes supporting footnote on controlling-sense; empty stub DROPPED |\n| Line 5 (¶ 2) | Second De Insomniis intro (cleaner prose) | CONSOLIDATE into Phase B; this is the lead version |\n| Line 7 (¶ 3) | Third De Insomniis intro + four-phenomena enumeration + rhetor's-strategy + closing claim | CONSOLIDATE into Phase B; closing claim PRESERVED verbatim |\n| Line 9 (¶ 4) | Methodological qualification | ABSORB into Phase C footnote |\n| Line 11 (¶ 5) | Synchronic/diachronic + two-mechanism analysis | DEVELOP in Phase D; this is the lead version of the structural argument |\n| Line 13 (¶ 6) | hexis + Geworfenheit connection (brief) | EXPAND into Phase E; this is the topic-statement; develop the full argument from later source lines |\n| Line 15 (¶ 7) | Phantasia conditions feedback (brief gloss) | DROP. Material redundant with §1.5/§1.6 phantasia treatment; not load-bearing for 1.7's argument |\n| Line 17 (¶ 8) | Summary statement; `\\textit{GeworfenheitI` typo | DROP as standalone paragraph (its content is captured in Phase E); FIX the GeworfenheitI typo if the formulation is redeployed |\n| Line 19 (¶ 9) | \"single shared phenomenon\" framing | ABSORB into Phase E movement (iii) |\n| Line 21 (¶ 10) | On Dreams 460b3–11 in perceptual register; coward example | ABSORB into Phase E movement (i) or (ii) — the coward's-fear example may be used briefly to illustrate Stimmung-saturation |\n| Line 23 (¶ 11) | BT §29 Befindlichkeit, thrownness, BT 174/178/390 quotes | DEVELOP in Phase E movement (ii); use BT 174 thrownness quote and BT 390 (= H.340) moods-temporalize-in-having-been quote |\n| Line 25 (¶ 12) | Rhetoric-as-first-systematic-hermeneutic (BT 178) + pathē-as-Stimmungen | DROP; cross-reference §1.2 (this material is established there) |\n| Line 27 (¶ 13) | hexis prefigures Geworfenheit; BCAP 122 quote | DEVELOP in Phase E movement (iii); central to convergence argument |\n| Line 29 (¶ 14) | \"What this analogy reveals\" — thrownness-character of chain | DEVELOP as Phase E closing-thesis (movement iii) |\n| Line 31 (¶ 15) | D.1/D.2 notation | DROP as letter-notation; the substance is preserved in Phase D |\n| Line 33 (¶ 16) | D.3 question | DROP as letter-notation; the substance is preserved in Phase D |\n| Line 35 (¶ 17) | D.3 = diachronic feature | DROP as letter-notation; Phase D states this in non-letter prose |\n| Line 37 (¶ 18) | 2 distinct phenomena in On Dreams (Stimmung-saturation; corrective-faculty impairment) | DEVELOP in Phase D |\n| Line 39 (¶ 19) | Mechanism (1) details + Mechanism (2) details | DEVELOP in Phase D |\n| Line 41 (¶ 20) | BT §29 three characteristics + lectures' four aspects | INTEGRATE into Phase E movements (ii) and (iii); preserve the three-characteristics enumeration cleanly |\n| Line 43 (¶ 21) | NE II.1 hexis-formation quotes | DEVELOP in Phase E movement (i); central to the Aristotelian articulation |\n\n====================================================================\nHIGHLIGHT / TYPO / FORMATTING CORRECTIONS\n====================================================================\n\nALL of the following must be resolved before output:\n\n| Loc. | Issue | Resolution |\n|------|-------|-----------|\n| Line 3 | `\\hl{(CITE)}\\footnote{}` empty footnote stub | DROP entirely; the citation it would have housed (*De Insomniis* 461b3–8) goes inline |\n| Line 17 | `\\textit{GeworfenheitI` (missing closing brace; capital I) | Corrected German: `\\textit{Geworfenheit}` (\"thrownness\") |\n| Line 11 | `subsequuent` | \"subsequent\" |\n| Line 11 | `loo` (in \"feedback loo\") | \"feedback loop\" |\n| Line 11 | `(1\\`)` (stray backtick) | \"(1)\" |\n| Line 23 | `wither` | \"whither\" |\n| Line 23 | `pirvate` | \"private\" |\n| Line 27 | `regist` | \"register\" |\n| Line 31 | `feedback` is fine but watch for `feeedback` elsewhere | \"feedback\" |\n| Line 37 | `gat` (in \"pass into doxa unopposed... pass through the gat\") | \"gate\" |\n| Line 41 | `wither` (second occurrence) | \"whither\" |\n| Line 41 | `feedback` is fine but `feeedback` would need correction | \"feedback\" |\n| Line 43 | `We for \\textit{hexeis}` | \"We form *hexeis*\" |\n| Line 17 | lowercase `stimmung` | `*Stimmung*` (German nouns capitalized + italicized) |\n| Line 23 | `disposition-already-having-been-shaped` | This is acceptable as a coined hyphenated compound; PRESERVE |\n| Line 9 | `On Dreams 460b3-11` (no italics on title in some locations) | `\\textit{De Insomniis}` consistently OR `\\textit{On Dreams}` consistently — pick one and use throughout; recommended: `\\textit{De Insomniis}` (Latin convention), with first-occurrence parenthetical \"(also translated as *On Dreams*)\" optional |\n| Throughout | Inconsistent capitalization of \"Stimmung\" / \"stimmung\" | Always capitalize as German noun; always italicize |\n| Throughout | The `\\ra` macro for right arrow | PRESERVE as `$\\rightarrow$` if `\\ra` is not defined in the document; otherwise PRESERVE `\\ra`. Match the rest of the chapter's convention. |\n\nCONSOLIDATION priorities (non-trivial):\n\n1. THREE duplicate De Insomniis introductions (lines 3, 5, 7) → ONE polished introduction in Phase B.\n2. TWO formulations of the synchronic/diachronic distinction (lines 11 and 37) → ONE polished formulation in Phase D.\n3. TWO formulations of the two-mechanism analysis (lines 11 and 37) → ONE polished formulation in Phase D.\n4. THREE formulations of the *hexis*/*Geworfenheit* parallel (lines 13, 17, 19, 23, 27, 29) → ONE coherent argument in Phase E with three movements (Aristotelian *hexis*; Heideggerian *Geworfenheit*; convergence at GA 18 p. 122).\n5. *Rhetoric*-as-first-systematic-hermeneutic content (line 25) → DROPPED; cross-reference §1.2 only.\n\n====================================================================\nCITATION FORMAT — HARD REQUIREMENT\n====================================================================\n\nARISTOTLE: Bekker notation only. NEVER use Barnes page numbers.\n\nCORRECT examples:\n- \"*De Insomniis* 460b3–11\"\n- \"*De Insomniis* 460b16–18\"\n- \"*De Insomniis* 461b3–8\"\n- \"*De Anima* I.4, 408b5–7\"\n- \"*De Anima* III.3, 427b21–24\"\n- \"*Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22\"\n- \"*Nicomachean Ethics* II.1, 1103a14–b2\"\n- \"*Nicomachean Ethics* II.1, 1103a16–17\"\n- \"*Nicomachean Ethics* II.1, 1103a31–b2\"\n\nINCORRECT — NEVER USE THESE:\n- \"Aristotle, *De Insomniis*, p. 730\" — FORBIDDEN\n- \"(Aristotle, *On Dreams*, p. 18)\" — FORBIDDEN\n- Barnes pagination of any kind — FORBIDDEN\n\nKNOWN ARISTOTLE BEKKER CITATIONS FOR THIS SECTION:\n- *De Insomniis* 460b3–11 — body-quote: \"We are easily deceived respecting the operations of sense-perception when we are excited by emotions…\"\n- *De Insomniis* 460b16–18 — supporting-footnote-quote: \"The cause of these occurrences is that the faculty in virtue of which the controlling sense judges is not identical with that in virtue of which images come before the mind\"\n- *De Insomniis* 461b3–8 — citation for the dreamer-ill-passionate-agitated phenomena enumeration\n- *De Anima* I.4, 408b5–7 — pleasure/pain/thinking as kineseis (cross-ref from §1.2; if cited, brief)\n- *De Anima* III.3, 427b21–24 — doxa produces emotion immediately (cross-ref from §1.4; cite-only, no re-development)\n- *Rhetoric* II.1, 1378a20–22 — judgment-affecting (cross-ref from §1.2; cite-only)\n- *Nicomachean Ethics* II.1, 1103a16–17 — \"moral excellence comes about as a result of habit\" (Phase E quote)\n- *Nicomachean Ethics* II.1, 1103a31–b2 — \"we become just by doing just acts…\" (Phase E quote)\n\nHEIDEGGER (BT): Cite by SZ German pagination AND English page. Format: \"(SZ §29, H.135; Eng. 174)\".\n\nKNOWN BT PAGES FOR THIS SECTION:\n- §29, H.134 / Eng. 172 — state-of-mind as ontically familiar mood (cross-ref from §1.2; cite-only if needed)\n- §29, H.135 / Eng. 174 — thrownness; \"we call it the 'thrownness' of this entity into its 'there'\"\n- §29, H.136 / Eng. 175 — moods disclosing Dasein \"prior to all cognition and volition\"\n- §29, H.137 / Eng. 176 — three essential characteristics of state-of-mind; prior-disclosedness\n- §29, H.138 / Eng. 178 — Rhetoric II as \"first systematic hermeneutic\" (CROSS-REF to §1.2 ONLY; do not re-quote in 1.7)\n- §29, H.139–140 / Eng. 179–180 — Befindlichkeit's ontological characteristics (use only if needed)\n- §68b, H.339 / Eng. 388 — temporality of state-of-mind: introduction to the analysis\n- §68b, H.340 / Eng. 390 — \"Moods temporalize themselves… modified by having-been\"; load-bearing for Phase E movement (ii)\n\nHEIDEGGER (BCAP / GA 18): Cite by translation page only — \"(BCAP, p. 122)\" or by section number — \"(GA 18, §17)\".\n\nKNOWN BCAP PAGES FOR THIS SECTION:\n- p. 119 — *hexis* as \"I am there, come what may\" (use only if directly supporting the temporality argument)\n- p. 122 — *hexis*-genesis \"in being-there itself\"; four aspects of being-there derived from *hexis* — load-bearing for Phase E movement (iii)\n- p. 125 — *hexis* as \"how of *pathos*\" (CROSS-REF to §1.2 fn. 1 ONLY; do not re-quote)\n- p. 173–174 — fear analysis (use only if directly supporting prior-disclosedness; not load-bearing here)\n- p. 176 — *pathē* as ground out of which speaking arises (CROSS-REF to §1.2 ONLY; do not re-quote)\n\n====================================================================\nSTYLISTIC CONSTRAINTS\n====================================================================\n\n- Lanham-tuned profile: avg ~31 words/sentence with periodic short-pivot variation; ~50% long sentences balanced by periodic short assertions; formality ~0.64.\n- Author-prominent citations at ~99% rate. Introduce Aristotle (or Heidegger) as agent BEFORE the quote: \"Aristotle observes,\" \"Aristotle argues,\" \"Aristotle states,\" \"Aristotle insists,\" \"Heidegger writes,\" \"Heidegger argues,\" \"Heidegger insists.\"\n- Transition vocabulary: *thus*, *specifically*, *indeed*, *accordingly*, *hence*. Do NOT use: *poignantly*, *strategically qualified*, *interestingly*, *it is worth appreciating*, *tellingly*.\n- Minimize meta-discourse: do NOT use \"to reiterate,\" \"before we can analyze,\" \"I should clarify here,\" \"as we have discussed,\" \"as I have argued above\" (use specific cross-reference instead, e.g., \"the prior section,\" \"the linear chain established in §1.6\").\n- Block quotes only when load-bearing — particularly the *De Insomniis* 460b3–11 passage in Phase B and the BT §68 H.340 moods-temporalize passage in Phase E. Extract decisive clauses elsewhere and unpack discursively.\n- Greek terms: italicized transliteration first, Greek script in parens — e.g., \"*hexis* (ἕξις 'settled disposition')\". Match the source draft's actual practice, not external prescriptions.\n- German terms: italicized German nouns capitalized — *Stimmung*, *Befindlichkeit*, *Geworfenheit*, *Gewesenheit*. On first use in this section, gloss in English in parens — \"*Befindlichkeit* ('state-of-mind' / 'how-one-finds-oneself-disposed')\".\n\n====================================================================\nVOCABULARY CONSTRAINTS\n====================================================================\n\nDO USE:\n- *De Insomniis* (Latin title; preferred over \"*On Dreams*\" for consistency)\n- *pathos*, *pathē* (cross-reference from §1.2; Greek + transliteration on first occurrence per section)\n- *resonant orexis* (cross-reference from prior chapter; do not re-define)\n- *doxa* (δόξα) (cross-reference from §1.4; do not re-define)\n- *phantasma*, *phantasmata* (cross-reference from §1.5/§1.6)\n- *aisthēsis* (αἴσθησις)\n- *hexis* (ἕξις 'settled disposition')\n- *kinēsis*, *kineseis* (κίνησις) — for the soul's being-moved\n- *Stimmung* (German: 'attunement' / 'mood')\n- *Befindlichkeit* (German: 'state-of-mind' / 'how-one-finds-oneself-disposed')\n- *Geworfenheit* (German: 'thrownness')\n- *Gewesenheit* (German: 'having-been')\n- \"controlling sense\" / \"corrective faculty\" — Aristotle's name for the discriminative apparatus disabled in *De Insomniis*\n- \"synchronic\" / \"diachronic\" — registers of the chain analysis\n- \"Stimmung-saturation\" — the first diachronic mechanism\n- \"corrective-faculty impairment\" — the second diachronic mechanism\n- \"thrownness-character of *pathos*\" / \"*pathos*-grade thrownness\" — the closing thesis-formulation\n- \"articulational concretion\" / \"articulationally concrete\" — the chapter's terminology, cross-referenced from §1.2\n\nDO NOT USE:\n- \"feedback loop\" without quotation marks at first introduction; the polished version should establish that the loop is a diachronic temporality, not a synchronic third direction. After the synchronic/diachronic distinction is drawn, \"feedback loop\" may be used as a convenient shorthand.\n- \"third direction\" / \"D1\" / \"D2\" / \"D3\" letter-notation (use \"the synchronic transitions\" and \"the apparent feedback direction\" or \"what appears to be a third structural transition\")\n- \"atmospheric attunement\" (§1.6's vocabulary, not §1.7's)\n- \"pre-cognitive\" without qualification (use \"pre-doxastic\" or \"pre-propositional\")\n- \"raw feeling,\" \"private inner episode\" — these are colloquialisms; the polished version uses Heideggerian/Aristotelian register\n- \"to put it another way,\" \"in other words\" — restate the substance, do not flag the restatement\n- \"as Heidegger would later say\" — Heidegger has already been cited in §1.2; cite directly\n- \"*Stimmung* or *mood*\" (oscillating between German and English) — pick one, stay with it; recommended: *Stimmung* on first use, gloss with 'mood/attunement' in parens, then *Stimmung* throughout\n\n====================================================================\nFORBIDDEN DEPLOYMENTS\n====================================================================\n\n- Do NOT re-establish the articulational-concretion thesis (established in §1.2; cross-reference only).\n- Do NOT re-establish the hermeneutical/apophantical \"as\" framing (established in §1.2; cross-reference only).\n- Do NOT re-establish the SZ §29 H.138 \"first systematic hermeneutic\" citation (established in §1.2; cross-reference only).\n- Do NOT re-quote the BCAP p. 125 \"*hexis* is nothing other than a how of *pathos*\" passage (established in §1.2 fn. 1; cross-reference only).\n- Do NOT re-quote the BCAP p. 176 *pathē*-as-ground-of-speaking passage (established in §1.2 Layer A; cross-reference only).\n- Do NOT develop the variable-presence cases (drinking case, habitual-rational case, evaluatively complex case) — established in §1.6.\n- Do NOT develop the somatic-preparation chain ($M_3 \\rightarrow M_4$) — established in §1.5; cross-reference only when forward-pointing to §1.8.\n- Do NOT develop the per-emotion definitions (anger, fear, pity, shame in detail) — established in §1.4; the coward's-fear example from §1.7 source line 21 may be used briefly as illustration, but no per-emotion analytic treatment.\n- Do NOT develop the enmattered-accounts framework (*DA* I.1, 403a25–b19) — established in §1.3.\n- Do NOT develop the *doxa*-gate's articulational-concretion function at length — established in §1.4; cross-reference only.\n- Do NOT develop fear at length as a master case (BCAP §21 fear analysis is a distinct thread) — out of scope here; the *Geworfenheit*-as-pathos-grade-thrownness thesis does not require it.\n- Do NOT develop the *Stimmung*-as-disclosive-medium argument at length (this is established in §1.2's BT §29 footnote work via the SZ §29 H.137 prior-disclosedness passage; cross-reference if the Phase E movement (ii) needs it).\n- Do NOT cite or develop *BT* §40 (anxiety) at length. Anxiety is a distinct *BT* analysis; brief reference for context is permitted only if it directly supports the thrownness-temporality argument.\n- Do NOT cite or develop *BT* §65 (primordial temporality of care) at length. §68b is the load-bearing reference for moods-temporalizing-in-having-been; §65 may receive a single brief sentence as a forward-pointer to the deeper temporality framework.\n- Do NOT cite secondary scholarship (White, Caston, Frede, Nussbaum, O'Gorman, Hawhee, Rickert, Gross, etc.) — they are not deployed in §1.7.\n- Do NOT include `\\inlinenote{}`, `\\hl{}`, TODO markers, or working-note markup in the output. The output is the final draft.\n- Do NOT generate \"Claim Map,\" \"Quotation Ledger,\" \"Citation Ledger,\" or \"Validation Summary\" sections as part of the body. If the pipeline post-attaches these as artifacts, that is acceptable; do not include them in the 2,300-word body count.\n- Do NOT use Barnes pagination for Aristotle.\n- Do NOT collapse Aristotle's text into Heidegger's interpretation — preserve the distinction between Aristotle's position and Heidegger's reading. When Heidegger is interpreted as recovering or extending an Aristotelian doctrine (e.g., *hexis* prefigures *Geworfenheit*), mark the mediation: \"the lectures' formulation of *hexis*…,\" \"Heidegger's reading,\" \"Heidegger's *hexis*-analysis.\"\n- Do NOT use Heidegger as the SOLE warrant for a claim about Aristotle's own doctrine. Aristotle must be cited first for Aristotelian doctrine; Heidegger is cited for Heidegger's reading of that doctrine. The convergence-claim in Phase E movement (iii) (GA 18 *hexis* prefigures *BT* *Geworfenheit*) requires both texts on the table.\n- Do NOT introduce the D1/D2/D3 directional letter-notation in the final output. The notation is internal scaffolding; the polished prose uses descriptive language (\"the synchronic transitions,\" \"the apparent feedback direction\").\n\n====================================================================\nBEKKER / SZ / BCAP CITATION LEDGER (use these exact references)\n====================================================================\n\nARISTOTLE — *DE INSOMNIIS* (*ON DREAMS*):\n- 460b3–1