ate-of-mind*, however, temporalizes itself *primarily* in *having been*. Moods temporalize themselves—that is, their specific ecstasis belongs to a future and a Present in such a way, indeed, that these equiprimordial ecstases are modified by having-been\" — Phase E movement (ii) load-bearing quote\n\nHEIDEGGER — *BCAP* / GA 18:\n- p. 119 — *hexis* as \"I am there, come what may\" (use only if directly supporting Phase E movement (iii); not load-bearing)\n- p. 122 — *hexis*-genesis \"in being-there itself\"; four aspects of being-there derived from *hexis*; the fourth aspect: \"Being-there must, *for itself*, take the opportunity to cultivate this being-composed as a possibility\" — Phase E movement (iii) load-bearing quote\n- p. 125 — *hexis* as \"how of *pathos*\" (CROSS-REF ONLY to §1.2 fn. 1; do not re-quote)\n- p. 176 — *pathē* as ground of speaking (CROSS-REF ONLY to §1.2 Layer A; do not re-quote)\n\n====================================================================\nDRAFTING PROCESS — MULTISTEP, NO INLINE VALIDATOR\n====================================================================\n\nUSE the multistep drafting pipeline (`investigateV1` / multi-step CLI). DO NOT use the inline-validator path.\n\nStep 1: Retrieve all relevant passages from `corpus/index/` (Bekker reverse-index, BT-structured, BCAP). Confirm Bekker / SZ-H / BCAP loci against the ledger above. Verify the *De Insomniis* 460b3–11, 460b16–18, and 461b3–8 passages (these are the section's load-bearing primary evidence).\n\nStep 2: Retrieve chunked content from ChromaDB collections for the loci. Cross-check against the index to ensure passage fidelity. For *BT* §68b (the moods-temporalize-in-having-been passage at H.340), verify the English translation page against the Macquarrie & Robinson translation in `corpus/rhetorical_ontology/Heidegger, Martin - Being and Time_(1962)_[My Copy].pdf` (the standard reference is Eng. 390).\n\nStep 3: Draft Phase A (opening hinge) and Phase B (the *De Insomniis